Continuing with Row 3.

Edit Program.PAL

Note: Since this is source level file maintenance, lower-case is allowed 
[or even welcomed].  The forthcoming P?S/8 SHELL [for P?S/8 version 9 
series] will support case RETENTION not case-sensitivity. [Those familiar 
with OS/8 will understand this directly].  When a file is created, the case 
of each character will be preserved.  When accessing the file, the case is 
ignored.  Directory printout routines can include appropriate options, such 
as:

1) output as is.

2) Force all upper-case.

3) Auto-capitalize the file name [otherwise presented in lower-case].

This example is used for two reasons:

a) To point out the aboce

b) Windows icon labeling uses proportional spacing.  If it was all 
upper-case, it wouldn't fit properly on the caption.  In some cases, 
deliberate space characters have been inserted into the text for cosmetic 
purposes only to force line breaks in multiple line captions.  It is not 
possible in the default Windows screen font to express PROGRAM.PAL on one 
line.

Program.PAL is a dummy file already in the Current_Program_Files 
directory.  This is a customized icons file.  As such, editing from these 
two icons works identically, but need not be related to the CHOSEN current 
file name as described earlier.  Thus, you can have open two editing 
windows [or tabs in tabbed editors] at the same time.There is no practical 
upper limit on open windows, but there can be only ONE designated as the 
Current File Name.  Thus, a user can use this file name and still not have 
to do "icon surgery" which is not for beginners. [I will document one in 
the manual, but it is a bit tricky; I will try to document the potential 
pitfalls, etc.]

As with all edit commands, you can close the file if you wish [and then run 
one of the operating systems].

Send Program.PAL

Edit WHATAMI.PAL
Send WHATAMI.PAL

These are analogous to the Program.PAL situation except the file name is 
WHATAMI.PAL  Additionally, the designated O/S is the latest P?S/8 [with the 
LPT: enabled\.  As such, the icons are medium blue by convention.  Note: 
WHATAMI.PAL is located in the Generic_Source_Files directory within the 
Source_Files section of the PEPS folders.  This is the main development 
area all users are encouraged to use.  [This makes it easier to transport a 
complete package with one backup operation.]

Edit FOCAL Program
Send FOCAL Program.

This is quite similar to the Program.PAL situation; the only change is the 
extension on the file [Program.FOC in the Current_Program_Files directory]  
As provided, this is an authentic copy of the original DEC HAMURABI King of 
Sumeria game completely un-modified.  The designated operating system is 
P?S/8 [with the LPT: enabled].  P?S/8 FOCAL can be passed a P?S/8 text file 
containing the entire program with such as:

.FOCAL HMRABI /G /E

By default, FOCAL lines are not echoed when the text file is the source 
[there are command-line switches to override this and more].

/G is used to automatically start after the input has ended.  /E is used to 
re-enable the = printout in the numerical calculations.  [Note:  This makes 
the output exactly match what is in an Introduction to Programming manual; 
however, many users consider it "ugly" so it is disabled by default using a 
patch created by the FOCAL, 1969 user community at large.  Since P?S/8 
FOCAL is binary-compatible with FOCAL, 1969, generally any of the original 
patches will work as expected; this is in contrast with the usual OS/8 
variants which lost all vestiges of compatibility, which is not only 
insulting to the user community [I remember some of them complaining in 
person!] but it shortchanges everyone because there are really good patches 
that just work. Actually, there are two of them built-in and a command-line 
option to turn them back to the default, etc.  It is strongly recommended 
for more info to read FOCAL-17, which is completely compatible and has a 
lot of good ideas; I have major binary add-ons that follow that good 
information, and builds upon the additional advantages gained from P?S/8, 
etc.

By PEPS convention, FOCAL-oriented project icons are a light blue.

Note: Additional customized icon pairs exist as was discussed earlier in 
the More Custom Programs separate directory window.  It is conceivable that 
icon will be moved here for
consistency. etc.

Open Log File

SIMH sessions can optionally  be logged [and the log file is cumulative].  
The log file can be opened in your choice of text editor [even if logging 
is currently disabled].

Note: It is recommended the log file be emptied periodically to not 
overflow the capabilities of the text editor chosen.

Setup

This opens a separate directory window of certain things that can be 
adjusted as needed. Among other things is your choice of one of three 
different text editors associated with the Edit commands, and a logging 
file management icon.  [You can disable or enable logging, and either 
preserve or clear the log file itself.]

Note: This might be swapped with the More Custom Programs icon as indicated 
above.

I'll close now to start a separate part 3 post.

cjl





As is the case with all Send operations, the current designated file 
Program.PAL [in the Current_Program_Files directory] is sent to SIMH as the 
PTR: file input stream.  The designated O/S is started up.  By convention, 
since the icon is black background, this means it is send to the OS/8 
booting SIMH. [Note: It is trivial to change which system' all of them are 
present in the corresponding batch file; just un-REM a different one and 
REM the present one to change it, etc.]






